    "clinical_columns_TMq9hH": {
      "type": "clinical-columns",
      "blocks": {
        "column_QMMNY3": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "top_icon_svg": "",
            "title": "Discipline",
            "icon": "check",
            "icon_svg": "",
            "items": "Cutting carbs\nIntense exercise\nDieting",
            "item_columns": 1,
            "bottom_text": "<span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">The problem</span>",
            "lightbox_text": "<h4><em>The problem was never your effort</em><em><strong>.</strong></em></h4><h4><strong>Cutting carbs </strong></h4><p>It lowers how much insulin you need, which is why the first few weeks felt like progress. But it doesn't repair the step that's broken.</p><h4><strong>Intense exercise</strong></h4><p>Exercise genuinely improves insulin sensitivity, and you should keep doing it. It also can't override a signalling problem.</p><h4><strong>Dieting</strong></h4><p>High insulin tells your body to store fat, and can make you hungry when you shouldn't be. You had to work twice as hard for half the result \u2014 that's a hormone problem, not a discipline problem.</p>",
            "card_bg": ""
          }
        },
        "column_b8LAeX": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "top_icon_svg": "",
            "title": "Supplements",
            "icon": "check",
            "icon_svg": "",
            "items": "Berberine\nAll-in-one hormone elixirs\nSpearmint tea",
            "item_columns": 1,
            "bottom_text": "<span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">The problem</span>",
            "lightbox_text": "<h4><em>They can work, just on the wrong thing.</em></h4><h4><strong>Berberine </strong></h4><p>Works through AMPK, a completely different pathway. If three months gave you modest results, that's why. It doesn't touch insulin, the real issue.</p><h4><strong>All-in-one hormone elixirs</strong></h4><p>Chasteberry, a dusting of six other things, and rarely a meaningful dose of any of them. Most work on androgens \u2014 which are downstream of insulin.</p><h4><strong>Spearmint tea</strong></h4><p>Has some real anti-androgen data behind it, and it's not a waste of time. But it acts on the symptom your insulin is producing, not on the cause.</p>",
            "card_bg": ""
          }
        },
        "column_C6R4bc": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "top_icon_svg": "",
            "title": "Tracking",
            "icon": "check",
            "icon_svg": "",
            "items": "Ovulation strips\nCycle tracking apps\nWearables & CGMs",
            "item_columns": 1,
            "bottom_text": "<span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">The problem</span>",
            "lightbox_text": "<h4><em>Measuring doesn't change it.</em></h4><h4><strong>Ovulation strips </strong></h4><p>You tested every morning. But a strip can only tell you whether ovulation happened \u2014 it can't make it happen.</p><h4><strong>Cycle syncing apps</strong></h4><p>Logging, charting, waiting for a pattern. The app assumes a cycle that's already working. It can't effect a cycle that isn't arriving.</p><h4><strong>Wearables & CGMs</strong></h4><p>It's genuinely useful information. But watching your glucose or cortisol spike in real time doesn't fix why it's spiking.</p>",
            "card_bg": ""
          }
        },
        "column_kXJYKW": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "top_icon_svg": "",
            "title": "What they said",
            "icon": "check",
            "icon_svg": "",
            "items": "The pill\nMetformin\n\"Just lose weight\"",
            "item_columns": 1,
            "bottom_text": "<span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">The problem</span>",
            "lightbox_text": "<h4><em>You were given management, not answers.</em></h4><h4><strong>The pill </strong></h4><p>It replaces your cycle with a withdrawal bleed and pauses ovulation entirely \u2014 the underlying problem remains, and now it's harder to see. Many women find that out when they come off it.</p><h4><strong>Metformin</strong></h4><p>It works mainly by reducing glucose output from your liver. Inositol works by supporting insulin-signaling pathways your cells use to manage glucose. Studies have found comparable outcomes for some PCOS-related measures, with inositol generally better tolerated.*</p><h4><strong>\"Just lose weight\"</strong></h4><p>The advice that ignores the reason it's hard. High insulin actively works against fat loss, so this asks you to fix the symptom while the cause keeps running.</p>",
            "card_bg": ""
          }
        },
        "column_ebLJLk": {
