    "category_list_grid_NDqtGH": {
      "type": "category-list-grid",
      "blocks": {
        "column_FWqteC": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Discipline",
            "count_text": "THE PROBLEM",
            "items": "Cutting carbs\nIntense exercise\n~Dieting",
            "lightbox_text": "<h4></h4><h4><strong>Cutting carbs </strong></h4><p>It lowers how much insulin you need, which is why the first few weeks felt like progress. But it doesn't repair the step that's broken.</p><h4><strong>Intense exercise</strong></h4><p>Exercise genuinely improves insulin sensitivity, and you should keep doing it. It also can't override a signalling problem.</p><h4><strong>Dieting</strong></h4><p>High insulin tells your body to store fat, and can make you hungry when you shouldn't be. You had to work twice as hard for half the result \u2014 that's a hormone problem, not a discipline problem.</p>"
          }
        },
        "column_JqccRw": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Supplements",
            "count_text": "THE PROBLEM",
            "items": "Berberine\nAll-in-one hormone elixirs\n~Spearmint tea",
            "lightbox_text": "<h4></h4><h4><strong>Berberine </strong></h4><p>Works through AMPK, a completely different pathway. If three months gave you modest results, that's why. It doesn't touch insulin, the real issue.</p><h4><strong>All-in-one hormone elixirs</strong></h4><p>Chasteberry, a dusting of six other things, and rarely a meaningful dose of any of them. Most work on androgens \u2014 which are downstream of insulin.</p><h4><strong>Spearmint tea</strong></h4><p>Has some real anti-androgen data behind it, and it's not a waste of time. But it acts on the symptom your insulin is producing, not on the cause.</p>"
          }
        },
        "column_8jAMWU": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Tracking",
            "count_text": "THE PROBLEM",
            "items": "Ovulation strips\nCycle tracking apps\n~Wearables & CGMs",
            "lightbox_text": "<h4></h4><h4><strong>Ovulation strips </strong></h4><p>You tested every morning. But a strip can only tell you whether ovulation happened \u2014 it can't make it happen.</p><h4><strong>Cycle syncing apps</strong></h4><p>Logging, charting, waiting for a pattern. The app assumes a cycle that's already working. It can't effect a cycle that isn't arriving.</p><h4><strong>Wearables & CGMs</strong></h4><p>It's genuinely useful information. But watching your glucose or cortisol spike in real time doesn't fix why it's spiking.</p>"
          }
        },
        "column_dTWyQ3": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What they said",
            "count_text": "THE PROBLEM",
            "items": "The pill\nMetformin\n~\"Just lose weight\"",
            "lightbox_text": "<h4></h4><h4><strong>The pill </strong></h4><p>It replaces your cycle with a withdrawal bleed and pauses ovulation entirely \u2014 the underlying problem remains.</p><h4><strong>Metformin</strong></h4><p>It primarily reduces glucose production by your liver, with a secondary effect on insulin sensitivity. But gastrointestinal side effects can make it difficult for some people to tolerate long term.*</p><h4><strong>\"Just lose weight\"</strong></h4><p>The advice that ignores the reason it's hard. High insulin actively works against fat loss, so this asks you to fix the symptom while the cause keeps running.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "column_FWqteC",
        "column_JqccRw",
        "column_8jAMWU",
        "column_dTWyQ3"
      ],
